*For primary & secondary schools*

## Primary & high school African drumming incursions

Students get hands-on with traditional African drums and percussion. As a result, they learn rhythmic patterns, practise active listening and build coordination. Then, as the rhythms layer up, the class creates an exciting musical ensemble, with traditional songs, call-and-response and dance woven in.

- Builds teamwork, communication, concentration and confidence
- Also develops appreciation of cultural diversity
- Explores the role of music and dance in West African communities
- Suits cultural, wellbeing and reward days, Harmony Week, as well as Music and Arts units

{% button
   label="Explore school incursions"
   href="/school-incursions/"
   align="left" /%}
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}
{% /section %}

*For teachers*

## How African drumming incursions support learning

Our African drumming incursions complement the [Australian Curriculum](https://v9.australiancurriculum.edu.au/) and the [Early Years Learning Framework](https://www.acecqa.gov.au/nqf/national-law-regulations/approved-learning-frameworks). As a result, they give educators a meaningful and joyful way to extend learning.

{% columns layout="equal" align="top" %}
{% column card="dark" %}
### The Arts: Music & Dance

Students explore rhythm, musical patterns and movement. They also develop creative expression, performance skills and an understanding of cultural traditions.
{% /column %}

{% column card="dark" %}
### General Capabilities

Each workshop supports Personal and Social Capability, as well as Critical and Creative Thinking and Intercultural Understanding.
{% /column %}

{% column card="dark" %}
### EYLF Learning Outcomes

For early childhood settings, activities connect with all five outcomes: identity, community, wellbeing, learning and communication.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}

{% section tone="grey" %}
{% columns layout="equal" align="top" %}
{% column card="none" %}
*Kindergartens & early learning centres*

## Kindergarten & early childhood incursions

For younger children, we make rhythm playful, active and full of discovery. Through simple drumming patterns, songs, movement and games, children explore sound, beat and rhythm. As a result, they develop listening skills, coordination, body awareness and self-expression. Most importantly, they have fun learning together.

So jump the big jump and dance the African dance rumble! After all, every child deserves the chance to belong, be involved and discover the joy of rhythm.

{% button
   label="Explore kindy incursions"
   href="/early-learning-and-kindergarten-incursions/"
   align="left" /%}

![Children drumming at a kindergarten incursion](/media/2019/02/IMG_2760.webp)
{% /column %}

{% column card="light" %}
### Supporting the EYLF through music, movement & play

Our incursions also align with the Early Years Learning Framework: Belonging, Being and Becoming.

1. **Identity & belonging**, because children participate, express themselves and enjoy making music together.
1. **Community**, since sharing rhythms, taking turns and responding to others builds relationships.
1. **Wellbeing**, as drumming and dancing support coordination, body awareness and confidence.
1. **Confident learners**, so playful exploration of sound and beat makes children curious, involved learners.
1. **Communication**, while children experiment, express ideas and communicate through music and movement.

In addition, the program introduces the richness of West African traditions, so children build respect for cultural diversity.
{% /column %}
{% /columns %}
{% /section %}

{% columns layout="equal" align="center" %}
{% column card="none" %}
![Students in costume drumming and dancing outdoors](/media/2019/02/lakescollege2.webp)
{% /column %}

{% column card="none" %}
